claims provider billing authority. A managed publish_artifact call synchronously reads its canonical ArtifactStore index row and rehashes the copied bytes at tool settlement, requires an exact public-metadata match, and binds the resulting readback digest to the durable call before the outer task returns. Forged metadata, missing or changed bytes, and partial readback-schema downgrade fail closed; ordinary task-result artifact arrays remain digest-only observations. Durable Cowork tasks enable managed workspace checkpoints, keep the runtime state directory outside checkpoint rollback, and synchronously re-read the independently validated transaction store at tool settlement. Committed and rolled-back evidence, terminal store state, coverage, external-side-effect scope, and the readback digest are bound to the durable call before the outer task returns; forged evidence, missing store state, and partial schema downgrade fail closed. Agent Core now exposes a content-free prepared transaction binding on the pre-tool boundary, and the durable started row binds its digest. If a process crashes after the checkpoint store becomes committed or rolled_back but before tool-result settlement, an explicit revision-bound command can recover only that child call from the exact terminal store record. Missing/nonmatching store authority, binding tamper, and replay fail closed, while the outer effect remains blocked for separate reconciliation. A declarative needsInput gate now parks a stage before its provider effect exists, persists one digest-bound request, blocks ordinary resume, and accepts only a bounded secret-screened answer bound to the exact request id and runtime revision. Re-execution reuses already-settled earlier stages and binds the accepted answer into the gated stage message; stale revision, option mismatch, request drift, and response replay fail closed. This is a CLI/runtime stage-boundary contract, not an in-flight provider elicitation or dual-IDE UI claim. Production runtime-status now reopens every stored artifact readback against the current ArtifactStore index and bytes and every bound checkpoint readback against the current validated transaction store. It reports digest-bound verified/mismatch/unavailable lineage without exposing content, so deletion, byte tamper, missing transaction state, and valid terminal drift cannot be hidden by the settlement-time cache. This proves status-time authority only: the stores remain mutable after projection, and neither WORM retention nor current workspace restore is claimed. Coverage remains explicit: partial checkpoints and tools with external side effects keep machine-readable gaps, and no full-workspace, external-system, immutable-retention, automatic outer-effect adjudication, or current restore claim is made. Pre-usage/pricing/artifact/checkpoint-readback-schema calls remain readable only with explicit unavailable, incomplete, and legacy gaps. Pre-durable-call states remain readable only through explicitly counted legacy result fallbacks that keep machine-readable gaps. This includes descendant calls whose original request source differs from their subagent attribution; operator reconciliation preserves the prewritten receipt. Spawned sub-agents and isolated skills inherit the already-persisted parent tool child-effect as their own workflow owner, so descendant provider/tool calls enter the same store with a verified owner chain; orphan owners, receipt or owner tamper, swallowed unknown outcomes, and outer provider-return settlement over descendant unknown are rejected. MCP calls additionally persist the outer/child identity tuple in their session ledger before transport and expose persisted start/settlement flags in the durable nested projection. Production session budgets now persist one exact authority per provider-call intent and settle only the matching known or unknown result across headless, REPL, WebSocket, sub-agent, and team execution. Unknown usage remains recovery-required instead of being charged as zero, while concurrent and late known settlements retain their own authority. Exact operator recovery can atomically combine verified provider usage records with explicitly abandoned non-usage authorities; verified token/USD accounting and marker finalization commit together, and persistence failure restores both totals and the recovery set. Every adjudication advances a digest chain that binds exact normalized usage records, authority ids, before/after totals, and the prior head. The durable sidecar now retains the canonical content-free receipt sequence as well as its summary head, validates receipt digests, predecessor links, totals continuity, and final totals on every restore, and exposes full records only through the dedicated budget receipts reader. Ordinary status retains only counts, digests, deltas, and receipt coverage. Legacy head-only snapshots remain readable and any new receipt is explicitly marked as a verified suffix rather than overclaiming a reconstructable prefix. These local records contain provider/model/token accounting metadata but no prompt, response, error text, or provider payload. They remain mutable local evidence, not provider billing proof, independent readback, or WORM retention. OpenAI request identifiers and receipts remain trace-only and are not independently readable. Provider billing/readback, native third-party idempotency, independent usage/receipt readback, immutable artifact retention, full checkpoint coverage, external-side-effect rollback, current workspace restore proof, automatic outer-effect adjudication, in-flight provider elicitation, dual-IDE consumption, plugin distribution, and the declared real-host exact-release matrix remain required for release readiness.",
